{
  "raw": {
    "https://haverford.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Haverford-Campus/Groundskeeper_R1459": {
      "title": "Groundskeeper",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Groundskeeper Job Description Summary Haverford College invites applications for our full-time (40 hours per week), non-exempt, benefits-eligible Groundskeeper opportunity. Job Description Total Compensation Hourly rate : $18.50 Benefits : The College offers a comprehensive benefits program that includes  automatic 10% retirement contribution to your retirement account upon eligibility (no match required), a 50% tuition benefit for dependent children upon eligibility, 14 paid holidays (including the week before New Year’s Day), affordable medical plans, Health Savings Account contribution of $700 for individuals and$1400 for families (no match required, available to employees enrolled in the High Deductible Health Plan), and a beautiful arboretum campus with walking trail and easy public transportation access. Please visit the Benefits page ( https://www.haverford.edu/human-resources/benefits ) for more information. Work Location : On-site About Facilities Management Facilities Management is organized around four core functions: Operations and Maintenance, Campus Stewardship, Planning and Construction, and Budget and Administrative Operations. This structure supports clear accountability, effective service delivery, long-term asset stewardship, and alignment of facilities resources with the College’s academic mission. Operations and Maintenance is responsible for the reliable day-to-day operation of campus buildings and building systems, including skilled trades, housekeeping, preventive maintenance, utilities, and customer service response. Campus Stewardship is responsible for the care, preservation, and enhancement of the College’s arboretum campus, including grounds, horticulture, sustainability-related landscape practices, and grounds equipment maintenance. Planning and Construction leads capital planning, design, renovation, and construction projects. Budget and Administrative Operations supports financial management, work order administration, project coordination, asset management, data, and reporting. Together, these functions position Facilities Management as both an essential service organization and a strategic partner in advancing the College’s mission, stewarding institutional resources, and supporting a safe, welcoming, sustainable, and well-maintained campus environment. About the Position The Groundskeeper performs grounds maintenance, seasonal operations, snow and ice response, event support, landscape care, and general exterior maintenance to support a safe, attractive, accessible, and well-maintained campus environment. This position contributes directly to the daily appearance, function, and stewardship of Haverford College’s arboretum campus. Reporting to the Grounds Manager, the Groundskeeper performs routine and special grounds work across campus, including mowing, trimming, planting support, leaf removal, snow removal, pathway care, debris cleanup, event preparation, storm response, equipment operation, and related exterior maintenance tasks. The position works closely with other Campus Stewardship staff, including Groundskeepers, Horticulturists, the Equipment Mechanic, the Plant Curator, and other Facilities Management colleagues. The successful candidate will be reliable, safety-conscious, service-oriented, and able to perform physically active work outdoors in a variety of weather conditions while supporting the College’s academic, residential, athletic, and community activities. The position is considered essential personnel and will be required to work during periods of inclement weather or weather emergencies when the College is closed or operating on a modified schedule. Primary Responsibilities Grounds Maintenance and Campus Appearance - 40% Perform routine grounds maintenance across campus, including mowing, trimming, edging, blowing, raking, debris removal, litter pickup, pathway cleaning, and general exterior care Maintain lawns, open spaces, pathways, roadsides, entrances, courtyards, outdoor gathering areas, and other assigned exterior spaces Assist with mulching, soil preparation, planting, watering, pruning support, weeding, turf repair, seeding, and landscape bed maintenance Apply fertilizers by hand, push spreader, or other appropriate application equipment, and assist with application of turfgrass products in accordance with applicable regulations and College procedures Assist with irrigation system operation, inspection, maintenance, troubleshooting, and repairs Assist with athletic field maintenance and preparation, including mowing, field setup, turf repair, and other seasonal maintenance activities Assist with routine grounds service requests and minor repairs to campus outdoor infrastructure, including walks, curbs, storm drains, roadways, minor hardscape, and other secondary exterior areas Remove leaves, branches, trash, and debris from campus grounds, walkways, roads, parking areas, and outdoor spaces Help maintain campus appearance standards, particularly in high-visibility areas used by students, faculty, staff, visitors, alumni, and prospective families Report damaged landscape areas, unsafe conditions, drainage issues, pests, vandalism, broken site furnishings, or other exterior concerns to the Grounds Manager Complete assigned work safely, efficiently, and in a manner consistent with the character of the College’s arboretum campus Seasonal Operations, Snow and Ice Response, and Storm Cleanup - 25% Participate in snow and ice response operations, including shoveling, plowing support, salting, sanding, clearing steps, pathways, ramps, entrances, roads, and other priority areas Operate snow removal equipment and use ice-control materials safely and responsibly as assigned Assist with storm cleanup, fallen limb removal, flooding response, drainage support, weather-related hazards, and emergency exterior conditions Support seasonal work such as spring cleanup, summer readiness, fall leaf removal, winter preparation, residence hall openings and closings, and commencement preparation Work early mornings, evenings, weekends, holidays, or extended shifts during snow events, severe weather, major campus events, or urgent operational needs Coordinate with the Grounds Manager and other team members to ensure campus spaces remain safe, accessible, and ready for use Equipment, Tools, Materials, and Safe Work Practices - 15% Operate and care for grounds equipment such as mowers, trimmers, blowers, utility vehicles, tractors, snow equipment, hand tools, power tools, carts, and related equipment as assigned Inspect equipment before use and report maintenance needs, damage, unsafe conditions, or performance problems Clean, store, and maintain tools, equipment, vehicles, work areas, storage areas, and grounds shops in a safe and orderly condition Use fuel, ice-control materials, soil, mulch, plants, tools, supplies, and other materials responsibly Follow College safety practices, equipment instructions, personal protective equipment requirements, and supervisor direction Participate in safety training related to equipment operation, lifting, weather exposure, traffic safety, chemicals, noise, tools, and other grounds-related hazards Campus Events, Projects, and Operational Support - 10% Assist with grounds support for commencement, alumni events, admissions programs, student activities, athletic events, move-in, and other campus activities Help set up, prepare, clean, and restore outdoor spaces before and after events Move, place, or remove outdoor furniture, signs, barriers, planters, materials, supplies, and related items as assigned Support landscape projects, site restoration, contractor work, construction impacts, and campus improvements as directed Coordinate with Housekeeping, Operations and Maintenance, Public Safety, Athletics, Student Life, Conferences and Events, and other departments as needed Work Orders, Communication, and Teamwork - 10% Receive, complete, and document assigned work through the College’s work-order system or other communication methods Communicate work status, supply needs, equipment issues, safety concerns, and campus conditions to the Grounds Manager Work independently or as part of a crew depending on the assignment Communicate respectfully and professionally with students, faculty, staff, visitors, supervisors, contractors, and coworkers Support a collaborative, inclusive, respectful, and service-oriented workplace Demonstrate reliability, punctuality, flexibility, sound judgment, and pride in the care of campus grounds Line of Report : The Groundskeeper reports to Grounds Manager. This position does not supervise other employees but may coordinate work with other Groundskeepers, Horticulturists, student workers, temporary employees, contractors, volunteers, and other Facilities Management staff as assigned. Minimum Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent Two years of experience in grounds maintenance, landscaping, parks maintenance, property maintenance, facilities operations, agriculture, construction, or a related field; or an equivalent combination of training and experience Professional Competencies Ability to operate or learn to operate grounds equipment, vehicles, tools, and snow-removal equipment safely Ability to perform physically active work outdoors in a variety of weather conditions Ability to understand and follow written and verbal instructions Knowledge of, or ability to learn, grounds maintenance, landscape care, turf care, snow and ice response, equipment operation, and safe work practices Ability to perform assigned grounds tasks safely, thoroughly, and efficiently Ability to identify and report unsafe conditions, landscape concerns, equipment problems, and maintenance needs Ability to work independently and as part of a team Strong reliability, punctuality, attention to detail, and willingness to respond to changing priorities Ability to communicate respectfully with supervisors, coworkers, students, faculty, staff, visitors, and contractors Ability to use radios, phones, mobile devices, work-order systems, or other communication tools as assigned Commitment to safe work practices, customer service, teamwork, sustainability, and responsible use of College resources Commitment to fostering an inclusive, respectful, collaborative, and service-oriented workplace Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education, public gardens, arboreta, independent schools, parks, municipalities, landscape companies, athletic grounds, or other campus-like environments Experience operating mowers, tractors, utility vehicles, snow-removal equipment, trimmers, blowers, chainsaws, or other grounds equipment Experience with snow and ice response, storm cleanup, seasonal grounds operations, event support, or landscape projects Basic knowledge of plants, turf, soil, pruning, planting, irrigation, drainage, or sustainable landscape practices Commercial driver’s license, current Pennsylvania Commercial Pesticide Applicator Certification, equipment-operation certification, OSHA training, chainsaw training, or other relevant credential Experience using computerized maintenance-management systems or mobile work-order technology Bilingual or multilingual communication skills We are aware that some people are less likely to apply for jobs unless they meet qualifications as described in the job advertisement. We are most interested in finding the best candidate for the job, and recognize that a successful candidate may come from a less traditional career trajectory, and have skills and abilities gained outside a classroom context, or an equivalent skill set. We encourage you to apply and share how you meet the position’s qualifications. Special Requirements Ability to obtain and maintain PA Act 12 background checks Possession of a valid U.S. Drivers’ License and ability to pass a Motor Vehicle Record check Ability to obtain and maintain a Pennsylvania Commercial Pesticide Applicator Certification within six months of hire. Reasonable additional time, up to twelve months from the date of hire, may be provided when an employee is actively pursuing certification and requires additional examination attempts This position is considered essential personnel and will be required to work during periods of inclement weather when the College is closed for a weather emergency Ability to work outdoors in a range of weather conditions, including heat, cold, rain, snow, wind, humidity, and seasonal pollen exposure Ability to respond to snow and ice events, severe weather, storm damage, campus emergencies, and urgent grounds needs outside normal business hours as needed Ability to work early mornings, evenings, weekends, holidays, or extended shifts when required by seasonal operations, weather, or campus events Ability to walk throughout campus, traverse uneven terrain, climb stairs, bend, kneel, reach, stand for extended periods, and perform physical tasks associated with grounds operations, with or without reasonable accommodation Ability to lift, carry, push, pull, move, or position materials, tools, plants, soil, mulch, snow, ice-control materials, and equipment consistent with the physical requirements of the position, with or without reasonable accommodation Ability to safely operate or work near vehicles, tractors, mowers, snow equipment, utility vehicles, power tools, and other grounds equipment Ability to work around plants, soil, pollen, insects, pesticides, fertilizers, fuels, noise, dust, and active landscape-maintenance operations while following appropriate safety practices To Apply : Interested applicants are invited to submit an application through Haverford College’s Workday career website (no other form of application is accepted). A complete application includes 1) a resume and 2) a cover letter summarizing your interest and how your experiences and skills meet the requirements of the position. Please email recruiting@haverford.edu with questions about submitting your materials. The application deadline is September 27, 2026 . Application review will begin after the deadline has passed. Should the search committee be interested in pursuing your candidacy, you will hear from them directly. Finalists will be asked to provide three professional references. Haverford College strives to maintain an equitable, accessible, and welcoming environment where staff, faculty and students of all backgrounds, perspectives, abilities, and experience can work, learn, and thrive. Haverford College is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate based on race, ethnicity, religion, gender identity, sexual orientation, national origin or ancestry, age, marital status, disability, veteran status, or any other identity or characteristic protected by law. Haverford College recognizes that we live and work on Lenape land."
    },
    "https://haverford.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Haverford-Campus/Post-Baccalaureate-Fellow_R1476": {
      "title": "Post-Baccalaureate Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Post-Baccalaureate Fellow Job Description Summary Haverford College invites applications for our full-time (40 hours per week), fixed-term (2 year), non-exempt, benefits-eligible, Hurford Center for the Arts and Humanities Post-Baccalaureate Fellow opportunity. Job Description Total Compensation Hourly rate : Hourly rate range starts at $20.00 Benefits : The College offers a comprehensive benefits program that includes automatic 10% retirement contribution to your retirement account upon eligibility (no match required), a 50% tuition benefit for dependent children upon eligibility, 14 paid holidays (including the week before New Year’s Day), affordable medical plans, Health Savings Account contribution of $700 for individuals and$1400 for families (no match required, available to employees enrolled in the High Deductible Health Plan), and a beautiful arboretum campus with walking trail and easy public transportation access. Please visit the Benefits page ( https://www.haverford.edu/human-resources/benefits ) for more information. Work Location : On-site. About Hurford Center for Arts & Humanities The John B. Hurford ’60 Center for the Arts and Humanities (HCAH) provides a place for inclusive and interdisciplinary programming by promoting collaborative engagement with the intellectual and artistic ambitions of Haverford College and broader communities. Advancing that mission of connection and inclusivity, the Hurford Center Post-Bac Fellowship is an entry-level position for someone with a demonstrated interest in the arts and humanities. About the Position The Post-Baccalaureate Fellow position has two primary components: 1) Exhibitions Support and Personal Program Development: The Post-Bac Fellow works with the Associate Director, Cantor Fitzgerald Gallery, VCAM (Visual Culture, Arts, and Media), and Campus Exhibitions to facilitate student, staff, and faculty-generated exhibitions in the College’s VCAM facility, the home and programmatic partner of the Hurford Center for the Arts and Humanities. The Fellow meets regularly with students, faculty, and staff to help conceptualize exhibition projects, later helping to install, deinstall, and otherwise facilitate these exhibitions, as well as support the Associate Director with similar work at the Center’s primary exhibition space, the Cantor Fitzgerald Gallery.  The Fellow also will receive financial and logistical support from the Hurford Center staff to stage projects that advance their own creative, academic, or professional pursuits. Funding and support will be available during the Fellow’s first year to organize public programs such as workshops, screenings, and other modes of campus engagement. During the second year, the Fellow will receive funding to stage a culminating project in VCAM, which may take the form of an exhibition, public event, or other creative project relevant to their interests. The Post-Bac Fellow will work with Hurford Center staff to develop this project. 2) Hurford Center Staff: The Post-Bac Fellow serves as part of the broader Hurford Center staff, generally assisting the Hurford Center team with event support, as well as attending staff meetings and serving on the Center’s Steering Committee, where they will review student and faculty grant applications. The Post-Bac Fellow also assists Hurford Center staff in mentoring the Center’s Student Advisory Board in reviewing and approving Student Arts Fund grants.    This is a two-year, full-time position, upon review by their supervisor at the end of the first year. Since some of the Center’s programming takes place outside of the regular workweek hours (evenings and weekends), the position requires some schedule flexibility. Candidates must have a valid driver’s license and will participate in college van certification as part of their role. Primary Responsibilities Work closely with HCAH staff in helping implement Hurford Center programs Work directly with Associate Director, Cantor Fitzgerald Gallery, VCAM, and Campus Exhibitions to install, deinstall, and otherwise facilitate exhibitions in VCAM Create Spaces Meet regularly with students, faculty, and staff to help conceptualize exhibition projects Receive funding to stage public programs in their first year and a culminating exhibition/event/project in their second Work closely with HCAH staff in helping implement major Hurford Center programs and events such as the Spring Gathering and Block Party Assist HCAH Assistant Director in student outreach for HCAH grants and programming as well as summer internships  Partner with the HCAH Assistant Director to organize the Center’s Philadelphia Artist Workshop Series  Assist in the facilitation of HCAH Student Arts Fund meetings Line of Report : The Post-Baccalaureate Fellow reports to Associate Director, HCAH and VCAM. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree An existing creative, artistic, or research practice Professional Competencies Comprehensive ability to coordinate multiple projects in dynamic group settings Ability to establish effective oral and written communication with internal and external stakeholders and constituents Adaptable thinker, with a motivation to learn and grow Knowledge of MS Office Suite Ability to work evening and weekend hours as programmatic needs dictate Willingness to complete campus van-certification upon starting role Preferred Qualifications Bachelor’s degree with a focus in the humanities or arts Experience engaging with artistic communities Experience in an academic/higher education environment or administrative office Familiarity with Adobe Creative Suite We are aware that some people are less likely to apply for jobs unless they meet qualifications as described in the job advertisement. We are most interested in finding the best candidate for the job, and recognize that a successful candidate may come from a less traditional career trajectory, and have skills and abilities gained outside a classroom context, or an equivalent skill set. We encourage you to apply and share how you meet the position’s qualifications. Special Requirements Possession of a valid U.S. Drivers’ License and ability to pass a Motor Vehicle Record check To Apply : Interested applicants are invited to submit an application through Haverford College’s Workday career website (no other form of application is accepted). A complete application includes 1) a resume and 2) a cover letter summarizing your interest and how your experiences and skills meet the requirements of the position. Please email recruiting@haverford.edu with questions about submitting your materials. The application deadline is September 22, 2026. Application review will begin after the deadline has passed. Should the search committee be interested in pursuing your candidacy, you will hear from them directly. Finalists will be asked to provide three professional references. Haverford College strives to maintain an equitable, accessible, and welcoming environment where staff, faculty and students of all backgrounds, perspectives, abilities, and experience can work, learn, and thrive. Haverford College is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate based on race, ethnicity, religion, gender identity, sexual orientation, national origin or ancestry, age, marital status, disability, veteran status, or any other identity or characteristic protected by law. Haverford College recognizes that we live and work on Lenape land."
    },
    "https://haverford.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Haverford-Campus/Social-Media-Specialist_R1458": {
      "title": "Social Media Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Social Media Specialist Job Description Summary Haverford College invites applications for our full-time (37.5 hours per week), exempt, benefits-eligible Social Media Specialist opportunity. Job Description Total Compensation Salary : Salary range begins at $ $57,900 Benefits : The College offers a comprehensive benefits program that includes possible work-from-home opportunities, automatic 10% retirement contribution to your retirement account upon eligibility (no match required), a 50% tuition benefit for dependent children upon eligibility, 14 paid holidays (including the week before New Year’s Day), affordable medical plans, Health Savings Account contribution of $700 for individuals and$1400 for families (no match required, available to employees enrolled in the High Deductible Health Plan), and a beautiful arboretum campus with walking trail and easy public transportation access. Please visit the Benefits page ( https://www.haverford.edu/human-resources/benefits ) for more information. Work Location : This position will be eligible for a hybrid work schedule, pursuant to the College’s Work Location Policy. About the Position The Social Media Specialist (SMS) is a hands-on content creator and channel manager responsible for leading the day-to-day management of the College’s institutional social media presence. The SMS is responsible for developing platform-specific approaches, creating compelling content, managing the social media editorial calendar (in partnership with the Editorial Director), monitoring and analyzing performance, and supporting effective social media best practices across the College. The SMS also monitors the College’s social media channels to help identify emerging issues and opportunities. Working within the College’s Marketing and Communications team, the SMS collaborates with colleagues and campus partners to identify story ideas, coordinate content, engage audiences, and strengthen Haverford’s voice and presence in the digital space. Primary Responsibilities Content Creation and Channel Management (60%) Manage the day-to-day operation of Haverford College’s institutional social media accounts, including scheduling, publishing, monitoring, and community engagement Create engaging, audience-centered content that brings Haverford’s people, academic experience, programs, values, and community to life Produce social-first content across formats, including short-form video, photography, graphics, written copy, stories, reels, and emerging formats Write and edit compelling social copy that reflects Haverford’s institutional voice while adapting appropriately to individual platforms and audiences Proactively identify stories, voices, events, and moments from across campus and translate them into relevant social content Attend and cover College events, programs, and campus activities as appropriate, including occasional evening and weekend assignments Collaborate with the Marketing and Communications team and other campus partners to develop high-quality multimedia content Cultivate engagement with Haverford’s social media audiences by identifying opportunities to participate in relevant conversations and build meaningful interactions around College-produced content Manage routine community engagement on institutional channels, responding to or routing questions and comments as appropriate Ensure social content reflects Haverford’s brand standards and commitment to accessibility and inclusive communications Editorial Planning and Platform Strategy (20%) In partnership with the Editorial Director, manage an integrated social media editorial calendar that balances College priorities, campus storytelling, audience interests, and timely opportunities Recommend platform-specific approaches based on audience needs, content performance, platform capabilities, and emerging trends Translate College communications priorities and content strategy into platform-specific social media plans, goals, and approaches, in partnership with communications leadership Recommend opportunities to strengthen, evolve, or adjust Haverford’s presence on individual social platforms Coordinate social content with broader College communications and marketing campaigns, initiatives, events, and announcements Stay current on changes in social platforms, algorithms, audience behaviors, content trends, and higher education social media practices and recommend appropriate adjustments Measurement, Insights, and Optimization (10%) Track and evaluate social media performance using platform analytics, social media management tools, and other available data sources Share regular insights with Marketing and Communications colleagues to help inform editorial planning and content development Establish and maintain practical KPIs and benchmarks for institutional social channels and campaigns in consultation with communications leadership Monitor measures such as engagement, reach, audience growth, video performance, referral traffic, and other indicators appropriate to College communications goals Analyze performance across platforms, campaigns, and content types to understand what is resonating with priority audiences Prepare regular social media performance reports that identify key results, trends, successes, and opportunities for improvement Translate analytics into actionable recommendations for content, publishing practices, platform strategy, and future campaigns Collaborate with marketing and communications colleagues to test new content formats, publishing approaches, and creative concepts and use results to inform future work Monitoring (5%) Use social listening and audience insights, when available, to identify emerging trends, conversations, and opportunities relevant to the College Monitor institutional social channels and relevant online conversations for emerging issues, reputational concerns, misinformation, significant community sentiment, and other matters that may require attention Recognize and promptly escalate sensitive, high-visibility, or potentially significant issues to communications leadership Social Media Best Practices and Campus Collaboration (5%) Help implement and reinforce the College’s established social media policy, standards, and best practices Maintain an inventory of official and affiliated Haverford College social media accounts, including account ownership and administrative access information Support sound account security and continuity practices, including appropriate administrative access, credential management, and adherence to College security protocols Serve as a resource for departments, programs, and offices managing College-affiliated social media accounts, providing practical guidance on content, platform practices, accessibility, brand standards, and account management Build relationships with social media contributors across campus to improve coordination, surface content opportunities, and encourage adoption of College social media best practices Identify opportunities to engage students and other members of the Haverford community as content contributors and authentic voices in the College’s social storytelling Line of Report : The Social Media Specialist reports to the Editorial Director and has no direct reports but manages up to three student workers who provide content for social media. Minimum Qualifications One of the following: Bachelor’s degree and two (2) years of relevant professional experience in social media, digital communications, content marketing, or a related field, OR Six (6) years of relevant professional experience in social media, digital communications, content marketing, or a related field Professional Competencies Demonstrated experience managing social media channels for an organization, institution, brand, or comparable entity Strong working knowledge of major social media platforms, their audiences, content formats, conventions, analytics, and evolving best practices; including content optimization Excellent storytelling skills; demonstrated ability to create engaging visual and multimedia social content, particularly photography and short-form video Experience developing and managing editorial calendars and coordinating multiple content priorities Experience using social media analytics to assess performance and inform content decisions Experience with Canva, Instagram edits, CapCut, in-app editing, and comparable creative tools Strong organizational and project management skills, with the ability to manage multiple deadlines and adapt to changing priorities Strong interpersonal and collaboration skills and the ability to build productive relationships with colleagues and partners across an organization Sound editorial judgment, discretion, and the ability to recognize when a social media interaction or emerging issue requires escalation Ability to work occasional evenings and weekends to support significant College events and activities Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education, nonprofit, or other mission-driven communications Experience with social media management, listening, analytics, and reporting tools Experience contributing to social media governance, account management standards, or organizational social media guidelines Experience in Adobe Creative Cloud Knowledge of digital accessibility and inclusive content practices Experience working with student content creators, interns, ambassadors, or other contributors We are aware that some people are less likely to apply for jobs unless they meet qualifications as described in the job advertisement. We are most interested in finding the best candidate for the job, and recognize that a successful candidate may come from a less traditional career trajectory, and have skills and abilities gained outside a classroom context, or an equivalent skill set. We encourage you to apply and share how you meet the position’s qualifications. Special Requirements Ability to work evenings and weekends as needed Willingness to monitor accounts outside of business hours as needed To Apply : Interested applicants are invited to submit an application through Haverford College’s Workday career website (no other form of application is accepted). A complete application includes 1) a resume and 2) a cover letter summarizing your interest and how your experiences and skills meet the requirements of the position. Please email recruiting@haverford.edu with questions about submitting your materials. The application deadline is September 22, 2026 . Application review will begin after the deadline has passed. Should the search committee be interested in pursuing your candidacy, you will hear from them directly. Finalists will be asked to provide three professional references. Haverford College strives to maintain an equitable, accessible, and welcoming environment where staff, faculty and students of all backgrounds, perspectives, abilities, and experience can work, learn, and thrive. Haverford College is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate based on race, ethnicity, religion, gender identity, sexual orientation, national origin or ancestry, age, marital status, disability, veteran status, or any other identity or characteristic protected by law. Haverford College recognizes that we live and work on Lenape land."
    },
    "https://haverford.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Haverford-Campus/Director-of-Planning-and-Construction_R1405": {
      "title": "Director of Planning and Construction",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Title Director of Planning and Construction Job Description Summary Haverford College invites applications for our full-time (37.5 hours per week), exempt, benefits-eligible, Director of Planning and Construction opportunity. Job Description Total Compensation Salary : Salary range begins at $126,360 Benefits : The College offers a comprehensive benefits program that includes possible work-from-home opportunities, automatic 10% retirement contribution to your retirement account upon eligibility (no match required), a 50% tuition benefit for dependent children upon eligibility, 14 paid holidays (including the week before New Year’s Day), affordable medical plans, Health Savings Account contribution of $700 for individuals and$1400 for families (no match required, available to employees enrolled in the High Deductible Health Plan), and a beautiful arboretum campus with walking trail and easy public transportation access. Please visit the Benefits page ( https://www.haverford.edu/human-resources/benefits ) for more information. Work Location : This position will be eligible for a hybrid work schedule, pursuant to the College’s Work Location Policy. About Facilities Management The Office of Facilities Management supports Haverford College’s mission by stewarding one of the nation’s most beautiful and historic liberal arts campuses. We are responsible for maintaining, renewing, and developing the physical environment that enables teaching, learning, research, residential life, and community engagement. Our team oversees campus planning, capital projects, facilities operations, maintenance, utilities, grounds, and sustainability initiatives. We are committed to providing safe, welcoming, efficient, and sustainable facilities while preserving the distinctive character of Haverford’s historic campus. Facilities Management is more than an operational department; we are strategic partners in advancing the College’s academic mission. We collaborate closely with faculty, staff, students, senior leadership, trustees, donors, architects, engineers, and contractors to deliver projects that enhance the campus experience and support the institution’s long-term goals. We seek professionals who value collaboration, thoughtful stewardship of resources, continuous improvement, and service to the campus community. Successful team members are committed to excellence, approach challenges with curiosity and creativity, communicate effectively with diverse stakeholders, and understand that every project contributes to the educational mission of the College. We value leaders who take ownership, make data-informed decisions, and ensure that every investment of the College’s resources advances institutional priorities and creates lasting value for the campus community. About the Position The Director of Planning and Construction provides strategic leadership and operational management for the planning, design, construction, renovation, and capital renewal activities of the College. This position is responsible for overseeing capital projects from concept through occupancy, ensuring projects support the institution’s academic mission, campus character, sustainability goals, operational needs, and long-term campus planning objectives. The Director serves as the College’s primary representative for capital project delivery and collaborates extensively with senior leadership, faculty, staff, students, trustees, donors, architects, engineers, contractors, and regulatory authorities. The role requires balancing strategic planning, technical expertise, financial stewardship, stakeholder engagement, and day-to-day project execution within the context of a small liberal arts college environment. Primary Responsibilities Capital Project Leadership Direct and manage the College’s capital construction and renovation program, including academic, residential, administrative, athletic, infrastructure, and campus landscape projects Oversee projects through all phases including feasibility, programming, design, estimating, bidding/procurement, construction, commissioning, occupancy, and closeout Establish project scopes, schedules, budgets, and performance expectations Ensure projects align with institutional priorities, campus master plans, and operational objectives Monitor project performance related to budget, schedule, quality, and risk Campus Planning and Strategic Initiatives Support implementation and refinement of the Campus Comprehensive Plan and related strategic planning efforts Evaluate space utilization, deferred maintenance priorities, infrastructure renewal needs, and long-range campus development opportunities Assist senior leadership in developing project priorities and capital planning recommendations Coordinate planning studies, feasibility analyses, and consultant-led assessments   Consultant and Contractor Management Lead selection, procurement, and management of architects, engineers, consultants, construction managers, and contractors Administer Requests for Quotation (RFQs), Requests for Proposal (RFPs), bidding processes, and consultant evaluations in accordance with College policies and governance expectations Negotiate professional service agreements and construction contracts in coordination with College leadership and legal counsel Foster collaborative and accountable relationships with external project partners   Financial and Administrative Management Develop and manage capital project budgets and cash flow projections Review and approve project invoices, change orders, pay applications, and project reporting documentation Coordinate with Finance on project accounting, forecasting, and reporting requirements Maintain comprehensive project records and reporting systems   Stakeholder Engagement and Communication Serve as liaison between Facilities Management and campus stakeholders throughout project development and execution Facilitate stakeholder meetings, design reviews, and project communication processes Prepare presentations, reports, and briefing materials for senior leadership and Board committees Coordinate donor-related project communication and engagement as appropriate   Regulatory Compliance and Risk Management Ensure compliance with applicable building codes, ADA requirements, environmental regulations, safety standards, and institutional policies Coordinate permitting, inspections, testing, and occupancy approvals Support risk mitigation and construction safety practices Maintain familiarity with higher education regulatory and compliance environments   Facilities Coordination Coordinate closely with Maintenance, Grounds, Environmental Health & Safety, and other Facilities units to minimize operational disruption during projects Ensure projects are designed and executed with long-term maintainability and operational efficiency in mind Support transition from construction to occupancy and ongoing facilities operations   Supervision Supervise and mentor the Assistant Director of Facilities Management for Planning and Design, and the Facilities Project Manager, including hiring, training, prioritizing work assignments, supporting professional development, setting goals, and conducting performance evaluations Foster a culture of mutual respect, collaboration, open communication, and accountability Line of Report : The Director of Planning and Construction reports to the AVP of Facilities Management. This position will supervise the work of the Assistant Director of Facilities Management for Planning and Design, and the Facilities Project Manager. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in Architecture, Engineering, Construction Management, Facilities Management, or related field Seven (7) years of progressively responsible experience managing capital projects, planning, design, or construction activities Three (3) years of experience supervising professional staff that report directly to you Professional Competencies Experience overseeing multiple concurrent projects with varying scale and complexity Demonstrated experience managing consultants, contractors, budgets, and schedules   Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education, institutional, or campus environments strongly Professional licensure or certification (Registered Architect, Professional Engineer, CCM, PMP, LEED Accreditation, etc.) Familiarity with historic campus environments and renovation of occupied facilities We are aware that some people are less likely to apply for jobs unless they meet qualifications as described in the job advertisement. We are most interested in finding the best candidate for the job, and recognize that a successful candidate may come from a less traditional career trajectory, and have skills and abilities gained outside a classroom context, or an equivalent skill set. We encourage you to apply and share how you meet the position’s qualifications. Special Requirements ●       Ability to obtain and maintain PA Act 12 background checks ●       Possession of a valid U.S. Drivers’ License and ability to pass a Motor Vehicle Record check To Apply : Interested applicants are invited to submit an application through Haverford College’s Workday career website (no other form of application is accepted). A complete application includes 1) a resume and 2) a cover letter summarizing your interest and how your experiences and skills meet the requirements of the position. Please email recruiting@haverford.edu with questions about submitting your materials. The application deadline is September 20, 2026. Application review will begin after the deadline has passed. Should the search committee be interested in pursuing your candidacy, you will hear from them directly. Finalists will be asked to provide three professional references. Haverford College strives to maintain an equitable, accessible, and welcoming environment where staff, faculty and students of all backgrounds, perspectives, abilities, and experience can work, learn, and thrive. Haverford College is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate based on race, ethnicity, religion, gender identity, sexual orientation, national origin or ancestry, age, marital status, disability, veteran status, or any other identity or characteristic protected by law. Haverford College recognizes that we live and work on Lenape land."
    },
    "https://haverford.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Haverford-Campus/Economics-Question-Center-Head-Tutor_R1468": {
      "title": "Economics Question Center Head Tutor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Title Economics Question Center Head Tutor Job Description Summary Haverford College invites applications for our part-time (6 hours per week), fixed term (8 months), non-exempt, non-benefits-eligible Economics Question Center Head Tutor opportunity. Job Description Total Compensation Salary $1,000 per month Work Location : This position will be eligible for a hybrid work schedule, pursuant to the College’s Work Location Policy. About the Position The Head Tutor oversees the operation of Haverford College’s Economics Question Center (EQC), a peer-tutoring resource for students enrolled in Economics courses. The Head Tutor is responsible for training, supporting, and supervising the student tutors who staff the EQC; communicating with faculty; and helping ensure that the Center provides a welcoming, effective, and academically supportive learning environment. The EQC operates on Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday evenings from 7:30–9:30 p.m. in Lutnick Library during the academic year. The EQC operates during the fall semester from approximately August 31 through December 11 and during the spring semester from approximately January 18 through April 30. The Head Tutor is expected to be present at the EQC at least one evening per week. Primary Responsibilities Tutor Support and Training Train tutors on effective peer tutoring practices, emphasizing helping students develop their own understanding and problem-solving skills rather than simply providing answers Provide supervision, guidance, and mentorship to tutors Address interpersonal issues, disputes, or other situations that arise among tutors or students using the Center, referring significant concerns to the Economics Department Chair as appropriate Promote a collegial, inclusive, and intellectually engaged environment in which students feel comfortable seeking assistance Faculty Liaison Serve as the primary liaison between Economics faculty and Question Center tutors, communicating faculty guidance, concerns, and relevant course information Center Administration Develop and maintain the weekly tutor schedule, including arranging coverage for absences and schedule changes Identify opportunities to improve the organization and effectiveness of the Question Center Collect and organize instructional resources for tutors, including practice problems, model solutions, and other materials provided by Economics faculty Line of Report: The EQC Head Tutor reports to the Chair of the Economics Department. Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree in economics or a related field or currently a doctoral candidate in economics or related field Professional Competencies Deep understanding of core undergraduate concepts in economics An understanding of how assigned problems fit into the broader economic enterprise Effective communicator with the ability to explain complex concepts in multiple ways Experience teaching To Apply : Interested applicants are invited to submit an application through Haverford College’s Workday career website (no other form of application is accepted). A complete application includes 1) a resume and 2) a cover letter summarizing your interest and how your experiences and skills meet the requirements of the position. Please email recruiting@haverford.edu with questions about submitting your materials. The application deadline is September 13, 2026. Application review will begin after the deadline has passed. Should the search committee be interested in pursuing your candidacy, you will hear from them directly. Finalists will be asked to provide three professional references. Haverford College strives to maintain an equitable, accessible, and welcoming environment where staff, faculty and students of all backgrounds, perspectives, abilities, and experience can work, learn, and thrive. Haverford College is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate based on race, ethnicity, religion, gender identity, sexual orientation, national origin or ancestry, age, marital status, disability, veteran status, or any other identity or characteristic protected by law. Haverford College recognizes that we live and work on Lenape land."
    }
